{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Remember what Amalek did to you.</b> The section of Amalek is juxtaposed to teach you that if you were false in weights and measures, you should fear the provocation of the enemy, as it is said (Proverbs 11:1): ״מאזני מרמה תועבת ה׳״ — 'A false balance is an abomination to HaShem' — and written after it: ״בא זדון ויבא קלון״ — 'When arrogance comes, then comes disgrace.' And it appears to me that because of this the verse ״כי תועבת ה׳״ was delayed, so that it was not mentioned immediately after ״אבן ואבן איפה ואיפה״ as it should have been — for had it done so, the section would have concluded with length of days. Therefore the Torah wished to conclude with ״כל עושה עול״ — 'all who do injustice' — in order to juxtapose it to Amalek, so that we may learn from the juxtaposition that the Holy One, blessed be He, gives the enemy dominion over anyone who does injustice in measures. And further, to teach you that Amalek did injustice to us and came against us from a distant land and did not remember the covenant of Elohim. And the expression ״אשר קרך״ is from the root מקרה (happenstance); this is its plain sense, and its midrash is ״צנן את היורה״ — 'he cooled the boiling bath.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:05.377273663Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}